Good. Now let me implement the fix: guarantee at least one head line when truncation happens. I'll modify:

```rust
// 40% head, 20% tail — gives more context at the top (imports, types, structs)
let head_count = (max_lines * 2) / 5;
```

to:

```rust
// 40% head, 20% tail — gives more context at the top (imports, types, structs)
// Guard: always keep at least one head line, otherwise tiny max_lines values
// (e.g. 1) would replace the entire content with just the omission marker.
let head_count = ((max_lines * 2) / 5).max(1);
```

Then tail_count:
```rust
let tail_count = max_lines / 5;
// Guard: if max_lines is too small for meaningful head/tail split, just take head
let tail_count = tail_count.min(total.saturating_sub(head_count));
```

With max_lines=1: head=1, tail=0, omitted = total-1-0. If total=1, we returned early (total <= max_lines). So here total ≥ 2, omitted ≥ 1. Good — head slice `&lines[..1]` is valid.

Wait — one more check: with the old code, could `head_count` ever exceed `total`? We return early if total <= max_lines, so total > max_lines ≥ head_count (since head_count ≤ 0.4*max_lines < max_lines < total). With the `.max(1)` change: head_count ≤ max(1, 0.4*max_lines). For max_lines ≥ 2, 0.4*max_lines ≥ 0.8, so `(max_lines*2)/5` could be 0 for max_lines=2 (4/5=0). `.max(1)` makes it 1. total > 2 ≥ 1. Fine. Slice is valid.

Now the marker text: `omitted = total - head_count - tail_count`. For max_lines=1, total=10: head=1, tail=0, omitted=9. Result: line 0 + marker "[... 9 lines omitted (10 total) ...]". Good — user sees at least one real line.

Now let me also write a regression test. Let me add a test after `test_smart_truncate_over_limit` or near the other smart_truncate tests:

```rust
#[test]
fn test_smart_truncate_tiny_max_lines_keeps_one_head_line() {
    let content = (0..10)
        .map(|i| format!("line {i}"))
        .collect::<Vec<_>>()
        .join("\n");
    // max_lines=1 is the degenerate case: head/tail rounding used to drop
    // the entire content, leaving only the omission marker.
    let (result, truncated, total) = smart_truncate_for_context(&content, 1);
    assert!(truncated);
    assert_eq!(total, 10);
    assert!(result.contains("line 0"), "head line must survive, got: {result}");
    assert!(result.contains("[... 9 lines omitted (10 total)"));
    // Tail is empty at this size; no panic, no marker-only output.
    assert!(!result.is_empty());
}
```

