Done. One small improvement, committed as `0936e68`.

**What I found and fixed:** `smart_truncate_for_context` (in `src/format/output.rs`) violated its own documented contract ("preserves head 40% / tail 20%") for tiny `max_lines` values. With `max_lines=1` or `2`, both head and tail rounded to zero, so the entire file content was replaced by just the omission marker — the caller got zero real lines.

**The fix (smallest correct version):**
• `head_count` is now `((max_lines * 2) / 5).max(1)` — at least one head line always survives when truncation fires. `tail_count` already guards with `.min(total.saturating_sub(head_count))`, so no overflow risk.
• Production call sites use `ADD_MAX_LINES` (500), where head=200/tail=100 are unchanged — zero behavior change on the real path.
• Added a regression test for the degenerate `max_lines=1` case (head line survives, marker counts correct).

**Verified:** `cargo fmt` ✓, `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings` ✓, `cargo build` ✓, `cargo test` ✓ (11 smart_truncate tests pass including the new one; full suite 4289 bin + 88 integration green). No doc updates needed — no documented behavior changed.
